1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,368
AET organizes fitness for practice into four domains. Consider what
you can safely do today, not just whether you have arrived or hold a
qualification.

2
00:00:10,368 --> 00:00:24,528
Cognitive readiness includes staying alert and following information.
Physical readiness includes safe movement and procedures. Emotional
readiness includes responding with judgement and respect despite
stress.

3
00:00:24,528 --> 00:00:35,736
Substance-related readiness concerns impairment from alcohol, other
drugs or medication effects. It does not mean that everyone using
prescribed treatment is unfit.

4
00:00:35,736 --> 00:00:47,880
One impaired domain can affect the others. Pain can distract you;
sleep loss can impair emotional regulation. Assess the function and
safety risk rather than relying on a label.

5
00:00:47,880 --> 00:01:01,272
Ask specific questions: can I concentrate on a handover, check a
calculation, move safely and respond respectfully? A vague feeling of
being fine is less useful than a functional check.

6
00:01:01,272 --> 00:01:15,144
Consider sleep and recovery, new illness, injury, emotional strain and
any impairing effect. If you cannot reliably perform an essential
task, address that before patient care or driving.

7
00:01:15,144 --> 00:01:27,384
Notify the appropriate supervisor and arrange a safe task handover or
other plan. Do not assume that a short break, a drink or a determined
attitude removes a serious impairment.

8
00:01:27,384 --> 00:01:39,336
Readiness changes during a shift. Repeat the check after deterioration
in concentration, a difficult event or an illness change rather than
treating the morning check as a permanent clearance.

9
00:01:39,336 --> 00:01:52,512
Describe the observed concern: repeated missed instructions or an
inability to stay awake. Do not turn these observations into an
unsupported diagnosis or an accusation about substance use.

10
00:01:52,512 --> 00:02:04,368
If there is an immediate danger, protect the patient and obtain
appropriate support. A polite private conversation is helpful only if
it does not postpone necessary safety action.

11
00:02:04,368 --> 00:02:13,920
Use the applicable supervisor and professional reporting process.
Reporting should be factual and limited to the necessary people and
information.

12
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:27,312
The school emphasizes support rather than blame, but a course cannot
promise absolute confidentiality or freedom from employment or
regulatory consequences. Ask how the actual process handles
information.

13
00:02:27,312 --> 00:02:36,768
During intensive study, notice whether you are still processing
information. Reaching the bottom of a page does not mean you can
explain or apply it.

14
00:02:36,768 --> 00:02:48,240
Increasing errors, lost concentration and irritability can signal
overload. Separate a learning difficulty from a readiness problem that
makes a practical task unsafe.

15
00:02:48,240 --> 00:03:00,744
Use focused sessions, planned breaks, movement and reasonable access
to food and water. Remove unnecessary distractions instead of trying
to combine study, messages and several videos.

16
00:03:00,744 --> 00:03:11,328
These measures support performance but do not cure severe sleep loss
or illness. If safety is affected, stop the unsafe task and obtain
support.

17
00:03:11,328 --> 00:03:24,312
Sleep alternates through non-REM and REM phases. Non-REM includes
three stages, with deep or slow-wave sleep in stage three. REM tends
to occupy more time later in the night.

18
00:03:24,312 --> 00:03:35,160
Sleep supports learning, memory and emotional regulation. The school
stresses protecting REM, but memory is not stored exclusively in one
sleep stage.

19
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:45,792
Plan a realistic sleep opportunity around work, travel and the
training weekend. Avoid routinely exchanging needed sleep for another
late-night reread.

20
00:03:45,792 --> 00:03:57,912
If sleep difficulty persists or alertness is unsafe, seek an
appropriate health assessment and a safe work plan. Self-care tips do
not replace evaluation of a continuing problem.

21
00:03:57,912 --> 00:04:08,568
Active recall means producing an answer from memory rather than
recognizing it on the page. Retrieval practice has experimental
support for improving delayed retention.

22
00:04:08,568 --> 00:04:17,712
Use the method on this week's work: explain a consent discussion, give
a handover or reconstruct a PCR sequence before opening the notes.

23
00:04:17,712 --> 00:04:30,240
Then check and correct. Repeating a wrong answer without feedback can
reinforce the wrong sequence. Practical skills also need observation
and coaching, not memory practice alone.

24
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:40,896
Space the next attempt over time and vary the case. There is no need
to predict the exact moment you will forget; use performance to adjust
the review interval.

25
00:04:40,896 --> 00:04:51,528
Use the school assignment list to decide what to study. Allocate time
to the actual required readings and practical preparation, not just
the easiest topic to revise.

26
00:04:51,528 --> 00:05:01,872
For example, review the handover structure early in the week, explain
it without notes later, then rehearse a new patient scenario with a
partner before class.

27
00:05:01,872 --> 00:05:10,848
Reserve manageable blocks around work and family responsibilities.
Tell others when you need study time and where you need practical
support.

28
00:05:10,848 --> 00:05:22,224
If the schedule is unrealistic, contact the instructor early and
prioritize safely. Repeatedly removing sleep is not a sustainable
solution to an overloaded plan.

29
00:05:22,224 --> 00:05:34,560
A single distressing event can produce an acute reaction. Repeated
workload, disrupted recovery and difficult decisions can also create a
cumulative burden without one dramatic incident.

30
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:46,368
Allostatic load describes wear associated with repeated stress
adaptation. It is a useful explanatory concept, not a diagnosis you
can establish from a colleague having a difficult week.

31
00:05:46,368 --> 00:06:01,944
Moral injury concerns distress when actions, omissions or witnessed
events conflict with deeply held values. Guilt, shame or anger may be
important features, even when the problem is not a single frightening
event.

32
00:06:01,944 --> 00:06:12,888
Recognize both the person's response and the conditions around the
work. A purely individual coping plan cannot remove unsafe staffing or
a recurring ethical conflict.

33
00:06:12,888 --> 00:06:24,168
Operational stress injury, or OSI, is a broad term for persistent
psychological difficulties related to operational work. It can include
different clinical conditions.

34
00:06:24,168 --> 00:06:36,504
Adjustment disorder, acute stress disorder and PTSD are clinical
labels, not stages that everyone passes through. Duration alone is not
enough to diagnose any of them.

35
00:06:36,504 --> 00:06:50,928
ASD concerns a defined pattern in the period from three days to one
month after trauma. PTSD has its own criteria and a longer duration
requirement; it does not require a prior ASD diagnosis.

36
00:06:50,928 --> 00:07:03,720
The learning task is to recognize change and obtain assessment, not
diagnose a teammate. PTSD should not be reduced to a claim that every
affected brain has a demonstrated structural lesion.

37
00:07:03,720 --> 00:07:16,584
Notice changes in sleep, unwanted memories, avoidance, mood,
concentration and daily functioning. The pattern and its impact matter
more than deciding whether the call was bad enough.

38
00:07:16,584 --> 00:07:27,864
Some reactions improve with time and support, but a person does not
need to wait a month to ask for help. Significant distress or unsafe
function deserves earlier attention.

39
00:07:27,864 --> 00:07:38,232
Offer a private, respectful check-in based on what you noticed. Listen
and help connect to an appropriate support or clinician rather than
demanding the full story.

40
00:07:38,232 --> 00:07:49,512
If there is immediate danger or inability to remain safe, arrange
urgent emergency assistance. Peer support is not a substitute for an
urgent clinical response.

41
00:07:49,512 --> 00:08:00,048
An operational debrief reviews how care and coordination worked. It
can identify an equipment problem or a communication gap and assign a
practical improvement.

42
00:08:00,048 --> 00:08:11,040
Psychological support addresses the effect on people, immediate needs
and access to further care. A discussion of tactics does not
automatically meet those needs.

43
00:08:11,040 --> 00:08:24,024
The purposes can coexist but must be clear. An operational review can
acknowledge distress without becoming therapy, and a support
conversation must not be a disguised performance investigation.

44
00:08:24,024 --> 00:08:36,312
Explain who is present, what records are kept and the limits of
confidentiality. Do not promise that every conversation is entirely
private regardless of immediate safety concerns.

45
00:08:36,312 --> 00:08:47,640
CISM means Critical Incident Stress Management. It is an umbrella for
support approaches; CISD, Critical Incident Stress Debriefing, is one
particular group model.

46
00:08:47,640 --> 00:09:00,168
AET describes defusing within eight hours and a more formal debriefing
around twenty-four to seventy-two hours. Learn these as the school
model's timing, not universal medical deadlines.

47
00:09:00,168 --> 00:09:10,944
Support may be appropriate after a colleague's injury, a distressing
child case, violence, a mass-casualty event or an apparently routine
case with personal impact.

48
00:09:10,944 --> 00:09:24,384
Do not require a particular event label before offering help, and do
not assume every exposed person needs the same intervention. Need,
consent, function and appropriate clinical assessment guide the
response.

49
00:09:24,384 --> 00:09:35,112
The school asks you to recognize seven Mitchell-model phases.
Introduction sets the purpose and ground rules; the fact phase
addresses roles and what happened.

50
00:09:35,112 --> 00:09:48,144
The thought phase concerns initial thinking. The reaction phase
concerns emotional responses. This distinction helps explain the
model's sequence, rather than mixing every type of response together.

51
00:09:48,144 --> 00:10:00,120
Knowing these labels does not train a student to facilitate
psychological trauma processing. Do not conduct an improvised group
session that pressures colleagues to disclose their most distressing
memories.

52
00:10:00,120 --> 00:10:12,648
The model is included because it is assigned teaching. Its use must
still be considered alongside evidence, professional competence,
informed participation and available alternatives.

53
00:10:12,648 --> 00:10:23,448
The symptom phase addresses effects such as sleep disruption and
distress. The teaching phase provides information about reactions,
coping and where to obtain further support.

54
00:10:23,448 --> 00:10:34,536
Re-entry closes the meeting with questions and next steps. Follow-up
matters because a brief session cannot establish that everyone is well
or that no further care is needed.

55
00:10:34,536 --> 00:10:47,328
For recall, group the sequence: introduction and facts; thoughts and
reactions; symptoms and teaching; then re-entry. Explain the purpose
of each, not only the name.

56
00:10:47,328 --> 00:10:57,120
A supportive follow-up can check needs and access to care without
requiring retelling. Learning the model does not remove a person's
choice about participation.

57
00:10:57,120 --> 00:11:11,592
There is an important evidence qualification to the school material.
NICE advises against psychologically focused debriefing for PTSD
prevention or treatment, because benefit is not established and harm
is possible.

58
00:11:11,592 --> 00:11:22,872
That does not mean abandoning a distressed colleague. Practical
assistance, a voluntary check-in, access to a clinician and a clinical
performance review are different activities.

59
00:11:22,872 --> 00:11:34,392
Do not promise that CISD prevents PTSD or require detailed disclosure
after every difficult call. Support should fit the person's needs and
the competence of the provider.

60
00:11:34,392 --> 00:11:46,656
For school learning, describe the assigned model accurately and
explain this qualification. For care, obtain an appropriate assessment
when distress or impaired function requires it.

61
00:11:46,656 --> 00:11:57,792
BCEHS publishes CISM and peer-support information and an employee and
family assistance route. Check the current official directory for the
access arrangements applicable to you.

62
00:11:57,792 --> 00:12:10,176
Its public page describes EFAP for PHSA employees and eligible family
members. Being enrolled in an AET course does not by itself establish
that employee eligibility.

63
00:12:10,176 --> 00:12:21,720
Students can clarify the school's support route and their own
available healthcare coverage. The BC First Responders' Mental Health
resource hub provides public education and support information.

64
00:12:21,720 --> 00:12:31,560
Ask about confidentiality limits and what to expect. Do not postpone
needed clinical help while searching for a perfect peer-support
programme.

65
00:12:31,560 --> 00:12:42,456
Resilience is the capacity to adapt and recover with support, not a
demand to be unaffected. It can include changing a plan or asking
someone else to help.

66
00:12:42,456 --> 00:12:52,512
Build ordinary recovery and relationships into the schedule. A support
network should include people outside the work identity as well as
peers who understand the job.

67
00:12:52,512 --> 00:13:02,448
A brief check-in can ask whether the person is safe, what they need
now and how to access support. It need not become a ten-minute forced
emotional retelling.

68
00:13:02,448 --> 00:13:14,208
Meaning and purpose can help some people, but no one owes the team a
positive interpretation of trauma. Persistent symptoms deserve care,
not a demand to think more positively.

69
00:13:14,208 --> 00:13:25,656
A learner or clinician has a responsibility to recognize limits and
act on a safety concern. An organization also has responsibilities for
the conditions in which people work.

70
00:13:25,656 --> 00:13:37,896
Workload, functional equipment, recovery opportunities and access to
support affect wellbeing. An individual cannot solve every recurring
system problem by becoming more resilient.

71
00:13:37,896 --> 00:13:49,392
If the same unsafe pattern keeps recurring, document and raise it
through the appropriate process. A personal coping strategy and a
system correction may both be needed.

72
00:13:49,392 --> 00:13:59,544
Supportive teams make it possible to speak early, arrange cover and
seek care. They do not demand silence in exchange for being regarded
as dependable.

73
00:13:59,544 --> 00:14:09,456
The school introduces post-traumatic growth as possible positive
changes after adversity, such as altered priorities or deeper
appreciation of relationships.

74
00:14:09,456 --> 00:14:19,008
It is different from symptom recovery. A person can find meaning and
still need treatment, or recover without describing the experience as
growth.

75
00:14:19,008 --> 00:14:28,368
Avoid using the term to pressure someone to be grateful for trauma.
The person's own account and needs matter more than a positive story
expected by others.

76
00:14:28,368 --> 00:14:40,176
For your plan, focus on actions you can influence: a readiness check,
a support contact, a realistic review schedule and a response if
function becomes unsafe.

77
00:14:40,176 --> 00:14:54,840
In this original example, the student has observable repeated errors,
not merely an unpleasant mood. Continuing an assessed practical task
without disclosure could place a partner or simulated patient at risk.

78
00:14:54,840 --> 00:15:06,072
The immediate response is to stop the unsafe part, tell the instructor
what is happening and arrange a safe plan. A request for help should
identify the performance concern clearly.

79
00:15:06,072 --> 00:15:18,768
Later, review sleep opportunity, scheduling and other possible
contributors with appropriate support. Do not diagnose the cause from
one sign or assume a coffee makes performance safe.

80
00:15:18,768 --> 00:15:30,648
A return plan requires a fresh readiness check and the instructor's
applicable process. The model explains safety reasoning; it does not
decide a school accommodation or pass status.

81
00:15:30,648 --> 00:15:40,032
AET asks for a personal resilience plan for the first year of
practice. Start with one real challenge and choose an action within
your control.

82
00:15:40,032 --> 00:15:51,192
One example is to schedule a brief voluntary readiness check with a
study partner before each practical session for four weeks, and review
whether concerns were recognized and acted on.

83
00:15:51,192 --> 00:16:02,040
Identify support and a review date. Record whether the action helped,
what barriers remained and whether an organizational change or
professional assessment is needed.

84
00:16:02,040 --> 00:16:14,448
A goal is a planning tool, not a promise to remain symptom-free. If
function becomes unsafe or distress requires care, use support
immediately rather than waiting for the review date.
